WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.369 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Those parts where
there was still progress

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:04.371
and where things were
still getting better

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:06.740
also were somehow distracting us

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
from the lack of
progress everywhere else.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.910 align:start size:94% position:6%
The ways in which we're
not a progressive society,

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
we use the word progressive,
gets used all the time,

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:15.015
but it doesn't stand
for actual progress.

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:18.952
And you know, I always say,
you know, the iPhones that,

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:21.621
you know, distract us
from our environment

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.723
also distract us from the
way it's strangely old.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:25.759
So you're looking at an iPhone
while you're riding a hundred

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:28.595 align:start size:97% position:3%
year old subway that's
completely busted in New York.

00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:30.096 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Right.
- And so there's

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:32.198
something about, we have
some elements of progress,

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.435 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they've been distracting
us from, you know,

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:38.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
the lack of progress or
even the outright decline.

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.508
(dynamic music)

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:49.382 align:start position:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin, live from
the local studio here in Miami.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.752 align:start position:0%
And joining me today is the
founder of The Thiel Foundation,

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:56.790
the co-founder of PayPal
and Palantir, Peter Theil.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.959
I could have given you like
a whole bigger intro there.

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:00.927 align:start size:88% position:13%
Anything else you wanna
throw in yourself?

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.529
- It's all good.

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:03.730 align:start size:81% position:19%
Generally, the
shorter the intro,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
the more flattering it is.
- Oh.

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:06.466 align:start position:0%
- You have super long, you have
a 20 page resume for people

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:07.700
who've never done
anything, so it's.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.402
- Well, I was gonna
say, Renaissance man,

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.872
disgruntled libertarian
something, something.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:14.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
- The longer the intro
gets, the more it suggests

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.508
that you're not really
doing anything at all.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:16.910
- Oh, all right.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:17.844
Well, you are doing a lot.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.379
I have, I actually have notes.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:20.547
I never have notes when I
do a show, but I was like,

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:21.848
I want to cover some new ground

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:24.317
and not just get into
the political thing

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:27.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we're always fighting
with everybody about.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.790 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I thought I'd start
because we are here in Miami.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:34.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, you famously
left San Francisco,

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:36.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
moved most of the
operation to Los Angeles.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.431
You do have a place in Miami.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.669
How do you feel about this
sort of movement of people

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:45.672
across the country right now
and sort of watching people

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:48.074
migrate to different
places to live very,

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:49.576
very different ways?

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:54.347
- Well, it's surely a very
healthy thing that, you know,

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:58.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's, in retrospect,
it's amazing that
people were as stuck

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:01.688
as they were in the
places they were in,

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:02.922
for such a long time.

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.791
And, you know, the
history of the U.S.

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:07.927
was that this had always been
a society where people moved

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:11.731 align:start size:84% position:16%
a lot between places
and the mobility,

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:14.400 align:start size:84% position:16%
the physical mobility
had actually gone
down probably a lot

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.704
for the last 40 or 50 years
relative to the, you know,

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.839
200 year history before that.

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:22.575 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so, and you know, it's,
it's probably the jury's still

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.445 align:start position:0%
a bit out whether this is a
temporary or permanent feature,

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:28.581 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's surely a
healthy recalibration.

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:29.816
It's sort of this idea,

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:32.752
you can always start
over in this country,

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:33.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
and one of the
ways you start over

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:35.688
is you move to a new place.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:36.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Were you kind of patting
yourself on the back,

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.958
that you were the first
guy outta San Francisco?

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:40.393
And my audience is well aware,

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:42.996 align:start size:94% position:6%
as I've posted some
videos from a recent visit

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:45.465 align:start size:97% position:3%
to San Francisco, the way
that place has just collapsed

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:48.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
under progressive policies
is absolutely insane.

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:49.836 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm guessing you don't
have any employees

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.704
that are wishing that
you guys had stayed,

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:53.940
although you still do have
some people there, right?

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.242
- There still are some
people, not very many

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:59.946 align:start size:88% position:13%
that still are living
in San Francisco proper.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:02.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
And yeah, it is
really extraordinary.

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:05.151 align:start size:88% position:13%
I lived in San Francisco
from 2003 to 2018,

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:12.158
and it sort of, you know,
it never quite got better.

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:14.227
But the idea, it took
a while for the idea

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.429
to sneak up on people
that it was actually

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:19.432 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the slow dk
deterioration thing.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:22.602
You know, the homelessness
was always a chronic problem.

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:26.306
Circa 2014, 2015, you
started to realize,

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:28.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, it's
actually getting worse

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.375
and they're never gonna,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.277
it's not just that this
is this fake problem

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:33.079
that they're taking
a long time to fix.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:35.381
It's, they are, it's a fake
problem they use to distract

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
from everything else,
they're never gonna fix it.

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.284
- So when you're hearing-

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:39.752 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But it's also a real
problem, you know.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.321 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, it's also right,
it's clearly a real problem,

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.289 align:start size:97% position:3%
but something that they
either don't seem interested

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:44.891
in fixing, well, what do you
think is the answer to that?

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.392
They're not interested?

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.228
- There are a lot of problems
that are both real and fake.

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:49.762 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- So the homeless

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:54.133
problem is, you know, yeah,
it's an incredible problem,

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:57.570 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it's also, you get a sense
that it never gets fixed.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.339 align:start size:81% position:19%
And so if you talk
about a problem

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:00.807
that you're never gonna fix,

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.976
then you can avoid talking
about all the other problems.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:07.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, let's say cost of living
for out of control rents

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.017 align:start size:97% position:3%
for people with homes or
broken schools or, you know,

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:12.986
crime or, you know, there's
sort of probably half a dozen

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:15.855
other issues that move to
the bottom of the queue

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:17.991
as long as we talk about
an unsolvable problem.

00:04:17.991 --> 00:04:19.459
- When you were there,

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.160 align:start position:0%
were you trying to talk to them
about those things and say,

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.197 align:start size:84% position:16%
guys like, look at
what is happening here

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:25.798
and the state of the decay?

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:29.869 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, on the city
level, it felt like exit

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:31.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
is much more
powerful than voice.

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:32.705 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- You know, it is.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:37.110
It was, I'm not sure
it's super corrupt,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
but San Francisco
is super ideological

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:44.350
in this very left wing,
very un-reformable way.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you know, it would
be, you know, it's,

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:50.023
I always have a schizophrenic
view about getting involved

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:52.392
in politics where it's
like super important

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:55.061 align:start size:84% position:16%
and super toxic,
but getting involved

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:59.265 align:start size:88% position:13%
in San Francisco City
politics, that would be,

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:01.267
that would be absolutely an
insane thing to do relative

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.535
to just moving.

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:03.903
- You're not enough of
a masochist for that.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.973
- It's, you know,
heroism's good.

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:08.374
Martyrdom not so good.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:11.978 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, and, and
yeah, the relative,

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:15.181 align:start size:97% position:3%
the relative sanity of getting
involved in local politics

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:17.583
or just moving out
of San Francisco, you
should always move.

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:19.819 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So to that point,
one more thing on this.

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.688
So now you're here
in Florida, you know,

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:22.555 align:start size:84% position:16%
you have a place here.
- For the winter,

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:24.157
last few winters, yes.

00:05:24.157 --> 00:05:25.425
- So you're splitting time,

00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:26.893
and obviously you also
have your place in LA.

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:29.796
But do you feel a real tangible
difference when you're here?

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:31.497
I mean, you know, I left
a year ago and it's like,

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.367
I have not looked back
and I'm loving it here,

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:37.003 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I see something
so incredibly powerful

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.237
and flourishing here.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:39.906
Do you feel that
when you're here?

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:42.075
- Well, it has, you know,

00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:44.110
there is sort of just an
extraordinary difference

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.946
if you're in a place
where you just feel

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:48.981
it's growing versus not.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:50.950 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- And there's a sense

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.753
in which Florida, Texas,

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:57.623 align:start size:88% position:13%
have this dynamic of
where it's just growing.

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:00.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, every
storefront is full.

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are no empty stores,
you know, everything.

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:04.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's not, I'm not sure
it's quite booming,

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:08.368
but it feels, you know, it
feels healthy and growing.

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:10.603
And then, and then, you know,

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:13.172
much of California does
not quite have that feel,

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:14.974
even though, of
course, you know,

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.009
Silicon Valley's
been this odd place

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:19.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
where it was a,
you know, gold rush

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:20.646
and everyone was depressed.

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:22.415 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- Even so for the last decade.

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:25.251
So Silicon Valley had
a very odd dynamic,

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:28.888 align:start size:97% position:3%
whereas a crazy boom that
didn't actually feel that way

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:30.590 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you, you know,
walk down the street

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:34.594 align:start position:0%
and then certainly with the
COVID shock the last few years,

00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:36.129
it's quite different.

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:38.798
I still think California is
probably somewhat healthier

00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:41.467
than New York or, you know,
completely bankrupt states

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.403
like Illinois or, you know,

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:45.638
non states like Puerto Rico.

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:46.806
- Mm-hmm.
- But.

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:48.141 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Why do you think
healthier than New York?

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:54.347
- I think, I think there are
ways that the finance industry

00:06:55.782 --> 00:06:58.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
that New York is centered
on is more movable

00:06:58.151 --> 00:07:00.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
than the tech industry
in California.

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:05.725
And probably the very big
tech companies like Google

00:07:05.725 --> 00:07:09.929 align:start size:97% position:3%
and Apple, it's hard to
picture them actually moving

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:11.130
out of California.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
Whereas you can
picture the big banks

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:17.236
gradually moving
out of New York,

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:19.172 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there's something
about finance

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:21.107 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's been a little
bit more movable.

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:23.643
It also paradoxically
makes it more dangerous

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:27.480
for California, because
if things ever go wrong,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
they will be so bust.
- Right.

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:30.650
- It'll be like Detroit,

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.518
which thought that it had
these captive big three

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.887
car manufacturers
and could get away

00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:37.323
with very bad policies
in Michigan, Detroit,

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:38.558
for decade after decade.

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:40.026
And then when, you know,

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:42.695
when that industry finally
went south, you know, it was,

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:44.497
it was just unfixable.

00:07:44.497 --> 00:07:46.632
- Do you find that these-
- New York's in a worse

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:49.569
shape right now because
people are, you know,

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:51.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
relatively more
people are leaving,

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:54.907 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's easier for the businesses
to leave and then maybe,

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:57.777
maybe California if it's
not careful, you know,

00:07:57.777 --> 00:07:59.846 align:start size:88% position:13%
it will at some point
really go off the cliff.

00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:01.280
- Right.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you find that these
things sort of happen slowly

00:08:02.114 --> 00:08:03.483
and then very quickly?

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:04.851 align:start size:97% position:3%
So something like California,
it's like, you know,

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:06.652 align:start size:84% position:16%
Callie's lost almost
a million people

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:07.920
in these last three years,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.288
and a lot of them
are high earners.

00:08:09.288 --> 00:08:11.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, these are
people who are paying

00:08:11.257 --> 00:08:12.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
into the system
that's ever growing.

00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:14.694
At some point, somebody has
to look at numbers, right?

00:08:14.694 --> 00:08:16.762
And be like, none of
this works, you know,

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:18.030
or I guess maybe not, right?

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:19.866
It just continues
somehow, I suppose.

00:08:19.866 --> 00:08:21.801
- Yeah, I actually,

00:08:21.801 --> 00:08:23.703 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't know how many of
them were the highest earners

00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:25.671
in California the
last few years.

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:28.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think New York was a
little bit more that effect

00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:29.976
than California.

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:32.245
But yes, these things, you know,

00:08:33.312 --> 00:08:34.547
we have these odd dynamics

00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:36.782
where things go on
for a very long time.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.284 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're not ultimately
sustainable.

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:41.554
But, you know, there's some,
there's some way I often think

00:08:41.554 --> 00:08:44.190
that much of the 2000s and 2010s

00:08:45.591 --> 00:08:48.661 align:start size:97% position:3%
were this weird continuation
of the 1990s, you know.

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:51.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
The decades, there were things
that happened, you know,

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:52.865
you had 9/11, you had the
global financial crisis,

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:54.734
Trump election, Brexit.

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:56.702
There were some events that
happened in those 20 years,

00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:58.204
but it was surprisingly little.

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:01.107 align:start size:88% position:13%
December, 2019, I was
reflecting on the 2010s.

00:09:01.107 --> 00:09:03.976
And I realized there've
been no retrospectives

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.111
on this decade.

00:09:05.111 --> 00:09:06.779 align:start size:84% position:16%
What actually
happened in the 2010s?

00:09:06.779 --> 00:09:08.881 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, we had
marijuana legalization,

00:09:08.881 --> 00:09:10.416
we had "Game of Thrones,"

00:09:10.416 --> 00:09:13.953
and people fell into
their iPhones and then,

00:09:13.953 --> 00:09:17.156 align:start size:97% position:3%
but then it was somehow just
this thing that was sort

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:20.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
of a stretched, exhausted
version of the 2000s,

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:21.994
which themselves
were a stretched,

00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:23.796
exhausted version
of the nineties.

00:09:23.796 --> 00:09:25.698
And then, and then I, yeah,

00:09:25.698 --> 00:09:29.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
I wanna say that in some
sense, you know, March, 2020,

00:09:29.368 --> 00:09:34.373
when COVID hits, we
finally, you know,

00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:37.376
a lot of these things
finally accelerate.

00:09:37.376 --> 00:09:40.713 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you know, and we're
finally in the 21st century.

00:09:40.713 --> 00:09:41.981 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So that's actually
a great segue

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:43.449 align:start size:84% position:16%
to sort of where I
wanted to start today.

00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:47.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because years ago, once
off camera, you said to me,

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:49.055 align:start size:97% position:3%
"I wouldn't be a libertarian
if any of it worked."

00:09:49.055 --> 00:09:51.691
And I just thought that
line pretty much captures

00:09:51.691 --> 00:09:54.293 align:start size:97% position:3%
so much of what's happening,
even right this moment.

00:09:54.293 --> 00:09:55.828
You referenced the last
three years of COVID

00:09:55.828 --> 00:09:58.831 align:start size:97% position:3%
where it seems like nothing
really works anymore.

00:09:58.831 --> 00:10:00.967
Our government kind
of doesn't work,

00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:03.536 align:start size:97% position:3%
our educational institutions
don't seem to work.

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:06.672 align:start size:84% position:16%
The medical field
doesn't seem to work.

00:10:06.672 --> 00:10:08.407
Is it all, is it sort of obvious

00:10:08.407 --> 00:10:11.143
that they were all not gonna
work at the exact same time?

00:10:11.143 --> 00:10:13.512
Or how did this happen?

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:15.314
- Well, you know, I'm
tempted to say the rot

00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:18.217
has been building
up for a long time.

00:10:18.217 --> 00:10:20.119 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, you know, if
you define technology

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:23.122
as doing more with less,

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:25.358
so many of these institutions,

00:10:25.358 --> 00:10:29.295 align:start position:0%
educational and even healthcare
are kind of the opposite,

00:10:29.295 --> 00:10:32.965 align:start size:97% position:3%
where you get the same for
more or you get less for more.

00:10:32.965 --> 00:10:35.301
So it's the anti-tech definition

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:38.137
when you think of the public
schools probably in some sense,

00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:40.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
the quality of the
education is way lower

00:10:40.706 --> 00:10:42.808
than it was 50 or 60 years ago.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:44.310
The costs are way higher.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:47.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so it's, it is, you're
getting less for more.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.016
And then there are, you know,

00:10:50.016 --> 00:10:52.685
versions of this with
healthcare where maybe,

00:10:52.685 --> 00:10:54.887
maybe people are getting a
little bit better healthcare

00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:57.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
than they were 30 years
ago but at double the cost.

00:10:57.189 --> 00:10:59.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's, again,
you know, a lot of,

00:10:59.492 --> 00:11:01.293
it's sort of like
a 80% socialist

00:11:01.293 --> 00:11:03.095
healthcare system that we have.

00:11:03.095 --> 00:11:04.597
Not a hundred percent, but 80%.

00:11:04.597 --> 00:11:07.299
And there's a lot of stuff
that's screwed up with that.

00:11:07.299 --> 00:11:10.503
So yeah, I think there are a
lot of things that had not been

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:13.172
working for quite some time.

00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:15.741
And maybe the interesting
question is why people

00:11:15.741 --> 00:11:17.410
weren't noticing it or
something like that.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.445
- Yeah, so what do
you think that is?

00:11:19.445 --> 00:11:20.646
Is that just our modern lives,

00:11:20.646 --> 00:11:21.814
we're staring at
our phones all day,

00:11:21.814 --> 00:11:23.516
we're watching TV shows

00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:25.651 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we're just not paying
attention to what's going on.

00:11:25.651 --> 00:11:27.953
- There were still some
parts of our society

00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:29.955
where things were progressing.

00:11:29.955 --> 00:11:32.358 align:start size:84% position:16%
There was certainly
some, you know,

00:11:32.358 --> 00:11:35.461
maybe narrow cone of progress
around computers, software,

00:11:35.461 --> 00:11:37.697
internet, mobile internet.

00:11:37.697 --> 00:11:41.133
And then those parts where
there was still progress

00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:43.302
and where things were
still getting better

00:11:43.302 --> 00:11:46.672 align:start size:97% position:3%
also were somehow
distracting us from the lack

00:11:46.672 --> 00:11:48.207
of progress everywhere else.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:49.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
The ways in which we're
not a progressive society,

00:11:49.875 --> 00:11:51.377
we use the word progressive.

00:11:51.377 --> 00:11:52.678
It gets used all the time,

00:11:52.678 --> 00:11:54.246
but it doesn't stand
for actual progress.

00:11:54.246 --> 00:11:57.950
And you know, I always say,
you know, the iPhones that,

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.519
you know, distract us
from our environment

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:02.588
also distract us from the
way it's strangely old.

00:12:02.588 --> 00:12:04.623
So you're looking at an iPhone
while you're riding a hundred

00:12:04.623 --> 00:12:07.526 align:start size:97% position:3%
year old subway that's
completely busted in New York.

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:09.028 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Right.
- And so there's

00:12:09.028 --> 00:12:11.063
something about, we have
some elements of progress,

00:12:11.063 --> 00:12:14.400 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they've been distracting
us from, you know,

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
the lack of progress or
even the outright decline.

00:12:17.703 --> 00:12:20.506
And then it, yeah, there
was some kind of crazy

00:12:20.506 --> 00:12:24.043
crystallizing event like
COVID where, you know,

00:12:24.043 --> 00:12:27.646 align:start size:84% position:16%
you have no science,
no rationality.

00:12:29.381 --> 00:12:31.917
You know, you can't, it
takes a long time to even

00:12:31.917 --> 00:12:33.552
get the vaccine approved.

00:12:33.552 --> 00:12:34.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, the FDA's
just a blocker.

00:12:34.987 --> 00:12:38.290 align:start size:97% position:3%
All these things, you
know, don't work that well.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:39.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
They still work relatively
better in the U.S.

00:12:39.792 --> 00:12:43.262
than many other countries,
but all sorts of things are,

00:12:43.262 --> 00:12:44.764
are really off.

00:12:44.764 --> 00:12:45.998
- So as the guy that was
the first outside investor

00:12:45.998 --> 00:12:47.633 align:start size:81% position:19%
in Facebook and
sort of, you know,

00:12:47.633 --> 00:12:51.070
at the beginning of the tech
boom 20 something years ago,

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:52.872 align:start size:88% position:13%
were you thinking about
some of that then?

00:12:52.872 --> 00:12:54.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or was anyone talking
about the fact

00:12:54.607 --> 00:12:57.810 align:start position:0%
that we might all get distracted
by so much information

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.279 align:start position:0%
and so much nonsense in
scrolling and all of this stuff

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:02.481
that everything else will
just kind of slide away

00:13:02.481 --> 00:13:03.649
and we won't even know?

00:13:03.649 --> 00:13:05.684 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like was there any
inkling of some of that?

00:13:05.684 --> 00:13:07.987
- Well, I didn't, you know,

00:13:07.987 --> 00:13:12.591
I think it's always a little
bit unfair to put too much

00:13:12.591 --> 00:13:14.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the blame on Silicon
Valley for this,

00:13:14.693 --> 00:13:17.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
where, you know, there
was some innovation

00:13:17.463 --> 00:13:18.898
in Silicon Valley.

00:13:18.898 --> 00:13:20.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was a sense in
which it probably was not,

00:13:20.766 --> 00:13:22.268
not quite enough.

00:13:22.268 --> 00:13:25.204
You know, there was a, this
manifesto that my venture

00:13:25.204 --> 00:13:27.907
capital fund put
out back in 2011

00:13:27.907 --> 00:13:29.575
where the tagline was, you know,

00:13:29.575 --> 00:13:30.810
they promised us flying cars

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.578 align:start size:81% position:19%
and all we got was
140 characters.

00:13:32.578 --> 00:13:36.649 align:start position:0%
And that wasn't, it wasn't meant
as an anti Twitter argument

00:13:36.649 --> 00:13:38.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
per se, you know, like
Twitter, you know,

00:13:38.784 --> 00:13:40.286
it's a good business.

00:13:40.286 --> 00:13:43.222 align:start position:0%
It's good for the several
thousand people that work there.

00:13:43.222 --> 00:13:45.858 align:start size:88% position:13%
Maybe there were
slightly too many, but-

00:13:45.858 --> 00:13:47.026
- Yeah, yeah.
- Or at least

00:13:47.026 --> 00:13:48.160 align:start size:88% position:13%
for the several
thousand that are left,

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.662 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's a good business.
- Yeah.

00:13:49.662 --> 00:13:52.364
- It's, and you know, it
somehow was transformative

00:13:52.364 --> 00:13:55.234 align:start size:97% position:3%
in some way, but it's
wasn't enough to, you know,

00:13:55.234 --> 00:13:57.336 align:start size:97% position:3%
take our whole civilization
to the next level.

00:13:57.336 --> 00:14:00.072
And this was, so I
think Silicon Valley

00:14:00.072 --> 00:14:02.174
was doing some things,
but it was not enough.

00:14:02.174 --> 00:14:05.010 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then, you know, there
were arguments that, you know,

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.846
it didn't have to all
be in Silicon Valley.

00:14:06.846 --> 00:14:08.681
You know, they weren't
building flying cars

00:14:08.681 --> 00:14:10.249
in Silicon Valley.

00:14:10.249 --> 00:14:11.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
They weren't building
flying cars anywhere else.

00:14:11.083 --> 00:14:12.551
- Right.

00:14:12.551 --> 00:14:13.786
So, since you mentioned Twitter,

00:14:13.786 --> 00:14:14.987
let's just do an Elon
thing for a second.

00:14:14.987 --> 00:14:16.522
As you watch the
guy buy this thing,

00:14:16.522 --> 00:14:18.557
obviously you guys did PayPal
together and everything else.

00:14:18.557 --> 00:14:20.693
Do you think he realized
what a freaking headache

00:14:20.693 --> 00:14:24.630
this thing was gonna become
and how crazy the product

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:26.498
under the hood actually was?

00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:33.005
- There was probably,
there was probably,

00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:37.610
man, I haven't talked to
him about the Twitter,

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:39.378
the Twitter acquisition.

00:14:39.378 --> 00:14:40.379
I think that-

00:14:43.015 --> 00:14:44.216
- Or just broadly speaking,

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:45.851
figure, finding that
so many of these things

00:14:45.851 --> 00:14:47.987 align:start size:88% position:13%
are sort of broken
under the hood in a way.

00:14:49.188 --> 00:14:53.125
- I think, I think
he had some idea,

00:14:53.125 --> 00:14:55.060
but probably not
the full extent.

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:55.995
- Yeah.
- Right.

00:14:55.995 --> 00:14:57.529
There was probably, you know,

00:14:57.529 --> 00:14:59.932
probably the fact that they
were willing to sell Twitter

00:14:59.932 --> 00:15:02.034
to him should have told him.

00:15:02.034 --> 00:15:05.337
They were just, I mean, it was
just, it was just, you know,

00:15:05.337 --> 00:15:06.906
Jack Dorsey, all
these other people.

00:15:06.906 --> 00:15:08.741 align:start size:97% position:3%
It was, you know, they
were just these figureheads

00:15:08.741 --> 00:15:11.744 align:start position:0%
and it was, I mean, the inmates
were running the asylum.

00:15:11.744 --> 00:15:13.112 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- And it was,

00:15:13.112 --> 00:15:16.315
it was probably on
some level, you know,

00:15:16.315 --> 00:15:18.150
you know, there was some part

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:19.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
of it that was
somewhat ideological,

00:15:19.685 --> 00:15:22.187
there was a way that Elon
felt like the wrong person

00:15:22.187 --> 00:15:24.290
ideologically to
take over Twitter.

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:28.260
But I mean, after a decade
of the stock going nowhere,

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.496 align:start size:84% position:16%
they were just
completely exhausted.

00:15:30.496 --> 00:15:31.764
- Yeah.

00:15:31.764 --> 00:15:32.998
You can only lose
money for so long.

00:15:32.998 --> 00:15:34.166
I don't know that much
about business, but.

00:15:34.166 --> 00:15:35.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
- They didn't lose
money, but they didn't,

00:15:37.102 --> 00:15:39.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you look at the
Twitter price the day,

00:15:39.872 --> 00:15:42.541
at the end of the first day
of trading, so, you know,

00:15:42.541 --> 00:15:45.344
price the IPO, it
closes on day one,

00:15:45.344 --> 00:15:46.812 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I forget what
the exact number was.

00:15:46.812 --> 00:15:48.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Oh, I think it was, it
was roughly the same, right?

00:15:48.814 --> 00:15:50.015
- It was roughly the same
as at the point where Elon

00:15:50.015 --> 00:15:51.617
offered to acquire it.

00:15:51.617 --> 00:15:53.018 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it had gone
nowhere in a decade.

00:15:53.018 --> 00:15:54.286 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- In a context

00:15:54.286 --> 00:15:55.287
where a lot of tech
stocks had gone up.

00:15:55.287 --> 00:15:56.455 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Right.
- So I think they were,

00:15:56.455 --> 00:15:57.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
they were just
completely exhausted

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.925
and it was sort of
a plea for help.

00:15:59.925 --> 00:16:02.895
And then Elon probably on
some level realized it,

00:16:02.895 --> 00:16:05.431 align:start size:97% position:3%
and on some level didn't
realize quite how, you know,

00:16:05.431 --> 00:16:06.932
please take this
company from us.

00:16:06.932 --> 00:16:08.968
And, and you know,

00:16:08.968 --> 00:16:10.069
you can hear that as
Elon, you're wonderful,

00:16:10.069 --> 00:16:11.270
you can do a great job.

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:13.672 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or we're just really,
really exhausted.

00:16:13.672 --> 00:16:14.840 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Right.
- It probably

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:15.674
was some combination of both.

00:16:15.674 --> 00:16:17.276
- Right, so, all right.

00:16:17.276 --> 00:16:18.310
So let's shift a little bit.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:19.778
I want to talk to you,

00:16:19.778 --> 00:16:21.280
one of the things that we've
covered an awful lot on my show

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:23.582
in the last two months is a
lot of the globalist stuff

00:16:23.582 --> 00:16:27.319
and the WEF and the
meetings in Davos

00:16:27.319 --> 00:16:28.787
and all of these things.

00:16:28.787 --> 00:16:30.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I always try to say
when I'm doing these things

00:16:30.889 --> 00:16:33.959
on the show, it's like I
don't have a full sense

00:16:33.959 --> 00:16:37.329
of how much influence
these organizations

00:16:37.329 --> 00:16:41.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
actually have versus just they
give these crazy speeches.

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:43.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
We all kind of
freak out about it.

00:16:43.035 --> 00:16:44.703 align:start size:84% position:16%
But what are actually
the policies?

00:16:44.703 --> 00:16:46.338
And then on the other hand,

00:16:46.338 --> 00:16:47.706
you see someone maybe
like a Justin Trudeau

00:16:47.706 --> 00:16:49.641 align:start size:88% position:13%
who seems like he
really is incorporating

00:16:49.641 --> 00:16:51.844 align:start size:84% position:16%
a lot of the
policies of the WEF.

00:16:51.844 --> 00:16:54.079
So as someone that, you've
been to some of these things

00:16:54.079 --> 00:16:55.547
over the years, right?

00:16:55.547 --> 00:16:58.183 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like what do you make of
what actually goes on there?

00:16:58.183 --> 00:17:00.552
I assume you're usually
kind of on the outside,

00:17:00.552 --> 00:17:01.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
even if you're there.
- Yeah.

00:17:01.787 --> 00:17:03.355 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Just because of your
political leaning.

00:17:03.355 --> 00:17:07.593
- I went to the WEF three
times, 2008, 2009, 2013.

00:17:07.593 --> 00:17:09.461
So I haven't been
in about a decade.

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:15.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
It is, I mean, there's things
about it that are maybe,

00:17:16.735 --> 00:17:19.938 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe talking something
about globalism generally.

00:17:19.938 --> 00:17:24.943
It is somehow this
official ideology.

00:17:26.145 --> 00:17:28.914
It is, it's in some
ways very exhausted.

00:17:28.914 --> 00:17:31.617
So it's, I think the
tide is going out.

00:17:31.617 --> 00:17:34.286 align:start size:88% position:13%
The high watermark
year was probably 2007.

00:17:34.286 --> 00:17:36.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Hmm.
- And it's been going

00:17:36.388 --> 00:17:37.890
out in some way for 16 years,

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:40.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's been going
out very, very slowly.

00:17:40.225 --> 00:17:45.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there are sort of
ways that it is, you know,

00:17:46.598 --> 00:17:49.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
in theory, you know, a
borderless, more integrated,

00:17:49.968 --> 00:17:54.740 align:start size:97% position:3%
more peaceful world, is a good
world for the 21st century.

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:56.175 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Theory, sure.
- That's good globalization.

00:17:56.175 --> 00:17:57.676 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Right.
- And then there

00:17:57.676 --> 00:18:00.012
are all kinds of versions
of it that are kind of bad,

00:18:00.012 --> 00:18:04.616 align:start size:97% position:3%
where it just ends up being,
you know, a racket for,

00:18:05.884 --> 00:18:07.686 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, dictators
stealing money

00:18:07.686 --> 00:18:09.922
and stashing it in
Swiss bank accounts,

00:18:09.922 --> 00:18:11.723
which probably with Davos
you can think of as a sort

00:18:11.723 --> 00:18:14.359 align:start size:88% position:13%
of reputation
laundering operation
or something like that.

00:18:14.359 --> 00:18:15.594 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- Or there are all

00:18:15.594 --> 00:18:17.629
sorts of versions
of it that are,

00:18:17.629 --> 00:18:21.333
you know, deeply,
deeply unhealthy.

00:18:21.333 --> 00:18:25.671
And I think it has been
on this kind of autopilot

00:18:25.671 --> 00:18:28.807 align:start size:97% position:3%
where it just keeps going even
though it's very exhausted.

00:18:28.807 --> 00:18:31.276 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think it's been
exhausted for 15 years.

00:18:31.276 --> 00:18:33.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Is that why in some
ways the rhetoric

00:18:33.312 --> 00:18:34.847
seems to ramp up
where, you know,

00:18:34.847 --> 00:18:36.949
they really are making it
sound like we control you

00:18:36.949 --> 00:18:38.150
and we are the gods.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:39.952
And it's sort of like hysterical

00:18:39.952 --> 00:18:42.454
because perhaps there actually
isn't, I mean, that would be,

00:18:42.454 --> 00:18:44.256
I would love that as
the takeaway here.

00:18:44.256 --> 00:18:47.693
- Yeah, there probably are
all these different vectors

00:18:47.693 --> 00:18:49.194
of globalization.

00:18:49.194 --> 00:18:50.996
There's, you know, trade
is the movement of goods,

00:18:50.996 --> 00:18:54.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
movement of people is sort
of immigration policy,

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:58.537 align:start size:88% position:13%
movement of money is
banking and finance and,

00:18:58.537 --> 00:19:00.506
and then movement of
ideas is the internet.

00:19:00.506 --> 00:19:01.974
And so there's sort of a,

00:19:01.974 --> 00:19:04.877
those are, you can sort of
analyze it in terms of these,

00:19:04.877 --> 00:19:06.979
these different sectors.

00:19:06.979 --> 00:19:09.081
And there were, in theory,

00:19:09.081 --> 00:19:10.883
all these ways these
things should work.

00:19:10.883 --> 00:19:13.252 align:start size:84% position:16%
In theory, you
know, free trade is,

00:19:13.252 --> 00:19:15.921
is a positive sum exercise
where both sides benefit.

00:19:15.921 --> 00:19:18.123
You know, I think it was, you
know, Adam Smith who said,

00:19:18.123 --> 00:19:19.758 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, why would
anybody ever throw rocks

00:19:19.758 --> 00:19:21.426
in their own harbor?

00:19:21.426 --> 00:19:26.365
And then, you know, being
able to move, you know,

00:19:26.365 --> 00:19:29.334 align:start size:88% position:13%
between countries and
places is also something

00:19:29.334 --> 00:19:32.137
that you might expect to see
in a dynamic healthy world.

00:19:32.137 --> 00:19:34.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
So there are sort
of all kinds of ways

00:19:34.473 --> 00:19:37.442
these things are in
theory pretty good.

00:19:37.442 --> 00:19:40.145
And then in practice
they went very haywire.

00:19:40.145 --> 00:19:43.882
The movement of money piece
was in some ways the global

00:19:43.882 --> 00:19:46.718 align:start size:97% position:3%
financial crisis where
people were sending the money

00:19:46.718 --> 00:19:49.354
to all these different
places all over the world

00:19:49.354 --> 00:19:50.989
where they had no
local knowledge

00:19:52.424 --> 00:19:55.961
and it was badly invested
and then the banks blew up.

00:19:55.961 --> 00:19:57.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that, so that
you can think of 2008

00:19:57.963 --> 00:20:02.267 align:start size:97% position:3%
as the financial part of
globalization kind of blew up.

00:20:03.435 --> 00:20:04.970
And then you know, one
version would be, well,

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:08.040
it's gonna just stop and we're
gonna stop sending the money.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.742 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it sort of got
replaced by governments.

00:20:10.742 --> 00:20:12.878
So if you think about Europe,

00:20:12.878 --> 00:20:15.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
sort of a mini
globalization in the form

00:20:15.314 --> 00:20:17.950
of the European Union, the EU.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.318
And basically in 2007,

00:20:19.318 --> 00:20:22.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
German savers were voluntarily
buying Italian bonds

00:20:22.187 --> 00:20:25.857 align:start size:97% position:3%
and sort of this
international financial flows.

00:20:25.857 --> 00:20:28.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
After 2008, nobody wanted
to do that anymore,

00:20:28.393 --> 00:20:30.896
but the Northern European
government stepped in,

00:20:30.896 --> 00:20:33.966 align:start position:0%
and started doing it and somehow
kept that game going for,

00:20:33.966 --> 00:20:35.534
for another decade or so.

00:20:35.534 --> 00:20:37.903
But yeah, my intuition is
that it's very exhausting.

00:20:37.903 --> 00:20:41.139 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's obviously a
China version of this.

00:20:41.139 --> 00:20:43.141 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- Where, you know, in 2007,

00:20:43.141 --> 00:20:45.978
people still talked about
globalization as, you know,

00:20:45.978 --> 00:20:47.779
all the developing countries,

00:20:47.779 --> 00:20:50.082 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they were gonna converge
with a developed world.

00:20:50.082 --> 00:20:51.617
And it was sort of,

00:20:51.617 --> 00:20:53.619 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was a sort of convergence
theory of history.

00:20:53.619 --> 00:20:57.589
And in some ways that
story got dominated

00:20:57.589 --> 00:20:59.691
much more by China.

00:20:59.691 --> 00:21:01.393 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there are, you
know, there are sort of,

00:21:01.393 --> 00:21:03.929
there are ways in which
China has been growing,

00:21:03.929 --> 00:21:06.498 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's actually
not been globalizing,

00:21:06.498 --> 00:21:09.001
if globalizing means
becoming, you know,

00:21:09.001 --> 00:21:12.738
sort of a, a western
liberal democracy.

00:21:12.738 --> 00:21:15.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so China's
actually, you know,

00:21:15.741 --> 00:21:17.309 align:start size:84% position:16%
this place that
hasn't been following

00:21:17.309 --> 00:21:18.577
that script terribly well.

00:21:18.577 --> 00:21:20.746
And if the biggest
country in the world

00:21:20.746 --> 00:21:22.514 align:start size:88% position:13%
doesn't fit the picture
of globalization

00:21:22.514 --> 00:21:24.349
at some point should tell you,

00:21:24.349 --> 00:21:25.350
you know, the theory's wrong.

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:26.418 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Right.
- End of history theory

00:21:26.418 --> 00:21:28.420
was a version of globalization.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:32.024 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I always say that,
you know, in 2000,

00:21:33.458 --> 00:21:35.861
you know, the end of history
itself was obviously over,

00:21:37.296 --> 00:21:40.832
ended in 2017 when Xi becomes
dictator for life, you know?

00:21:40.832 --> 00:21:42.167
- Right.

00:21:42.167 --> 00:21:43.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think-
- And the biggest country

00:21:43.268 --> 00:21:44.736 align:start size:75% position:25%
isn't gonna-
- I think Blake,

00:21:44.736 --> 00:21:46.305 align:start size:97% position:3%
Blake Masters, who you
co-wrote "Zero to One" with,

00:21:46.305 --> 00:21:49.241
I think his line on China was
we thought that we would make

00:21:49.241 --> 00:21:53.245 align:start size:97% position:3%
China more like us basically
by having a conversation

00:21:53.245 --> 00:21:55.480
with China about what's
going on with the world.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:57.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
And instead we became
more like China.

00:21:57.783 --> 00:21:59.251
So I take it you probably agree

00:21:59.251 --> 00:22:00.752
with that premise, generally?

00:22:00.752 --> 00:22:04.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It's a very disturb,
it's a, you know,

00:22:04.356 --> 00:22:07.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
it has a great deal of very
disturbing truth to it.

00:22:07.559 --> 00:22:11.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
And where yeah, there's
sort of all this, you know,

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:13.899
social credit scoring,
centralized control.

00:22:13.899 --> 00:22:17.736
Obviously we're still very
far ways off from China.

00:22:17.736 --> 00:22:20.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, I wouldn't
wanna move there.

00:22:20.405 --> 00:22:22.741
China is a lot worse than
China was 10 years ago.

00:22:22.741 --> 00:22:23.975 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Mm-hmm.
- I mean, you know,

00:22:23.975 --> 00:22:25.777 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think, you know,
I think it was,

00:22:25.777 --> 00:22:29.047 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, it was a one party
communist state in 2012,

00:22:29.047 --> 00:22:31.216
but I don't think it
felt as heavy handed

00:22:31.216 --> 00:22:32.751 align:start size:84% position:16%
and as totalitarian
as it does now.

00:22:32.751 --> 00:22:35.454
I mean, there's, I don't know
what the right metaphor is.

00:22:35.454 --> 00:22:38.090
It's like the Cylons in
"Battlestar Galactica,"

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:40.359 align:start size:88% position:13%
where they've just
been, the tech is just,

00:22:40.359 --> 00:22:41.860
it's like all the
surveillance tech.

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.930
Everyone's being monitored
at all times and all places

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:47.332
now in a way that they
were not a decade ago.

00:22:47.332 --> 00:22:49.101 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So is the white pill
a version of that,

00:22:49.101 --> 00:22:51.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it just can't
sustain itself long enough

00:22:51.069 --> 00:22:53.071 align:start size:81% position:19%
if you surveil
people constantly,

00:22:53.071 --> 00:22:54.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you control
everything constantly,

00:22:54.773 --> 00:22:57.242 align:start size:97% position:3%
eventually you cannot maintain
that level of control.

00:22:57.242 --> 00:22:59.010
Something to that effect.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:01.680
- There are, you
know, there are,

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:03.615
there are stories
we like to tell

00:23:03.615 --> 00:23:06.585 align:start size:81% position:19%
where it's just
going to collapse.

00:23:06.585 --> 00:23:08.553 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are, you know, there
are also very pessim-

00:23:08.553 --> 00:23:10.055
I think those are
too optimistic.

00:23:10.055 --> 00:23:12.791
And I think there are ones
that are, you know, overly,

00:23:12.791 --> 00:23:16.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
overly pessimistic where
China's just gonna take over

00:23:16.328 --> 00:23:17.763 align:start size:75% position:25%
the whole world.
- Yeah.

00:23:17.763 --> 00:23:20.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And somehow it's more
efficient or things like that.

00:23:20.165 --> 00:23:21.767
And I think both the
extreme optimistic

00:23:21.767 --> 00:23:24.403
and extremely pessimistic
stories are probably wrong,

00:23:24.403 --> 00:23:25.837
it's somewhere in between.

00:23:25.837 --> 00:23:29.207
And, you know, we have
to, we should not assume

00:23:29.207 --> 00:23:30.475
it's gonna collapse on its own.

00:23:30.475 --> 00:23:34.045
We need to think very
hard about, you know,

00:23:34.045 --> 00:23:37.349
you know, how we rise
up to the challenge

00:23:37.349 --> 00:23:39.284
that China represents,
and it has, you know,

00:23:39.284 --> 00:23:43.422 align:start size:88% position:13%
has all these
dimensions, military,
technological, economic.

00:23:43.422 --> 00:23:45.424
It's sort of much
more multifaceted

00:23:45.424 --> 00:23:48.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
than the challenge the
Soviet Union was, which was,

00:23:48.026 --> 00:23:51.930
you know, much more military
and you know, ideological.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.332
- When you say we, is it like,

00:23:54.332 --> 00:23:56.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
is it our political
establishment,

00:23:56.401 --> 00:23:58.637
that we are the ones that
are gonna have to deal

00:23:58.637 --> 00:24:00.138
with this probably?

00:24:00.138 --> 00:24:01.540
Like what actually is we now?

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:02.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
- We is always,
that's a good catch.

00:24:02.741 --> 00:24:03.942 align:start size:84% position:16%
We is always a very
ambiguous word.

00:24:03.942 --> 00:24:05.444 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- It means we conservatives,

00:24:05.444 --> 00:24:07.579
we libertarians, it
means we Republicans-

00:24:07.579 --> 00:24:08.914 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Right.
- We Americans

00:24:08.914 --> 00:24:11.316
and we the western world

00:24:11.316 --> 00:24:13.585 align:start size:88% position:13%
or we all the countries
that are not China.

00:24:13.585 --> 00:24:15.187
- Yeah.

00:24:15.187 --> 00:24:16.521
Or maybe we.

00:24:16.521 --> 00:24:17.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
The two of us!
- Or maybe the two of us.

00:24:17.989 --> 00:24:19.224
- Right, like, so, but
what do you mean by that?

00:24:19.224 --> 00:24:20.091
Like in a sense of
like if we were-

00:24:20.091 --> 00:24:21.726 align:start size:75% position:25%
- All the above.
- Yeah.

00:24:21.726 --> 00:24:23.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
- All the above.
- It's all the above.

00:24:23.195 --> 00:24:25.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
- You know, there's, there
probably is, you know,

00:24:27.165 --> 00:24:30.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's always a debate
between, let's say, you know,

00:24:31.937 --> 00:24:34.206
the President Trump's policy
was somewhat of a unilateral

00:24:34.206 --> 00:24:36.808
anti tough on China policy.

00:24:38.276 --> 00:24:39.845
And there's obviously a sense
where a multilateral approach

00:24:39.845 --> 00:24:43.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
to China is more
powerful and better.

00:24:43.682 --> 00:24:45.116
It's also hard to pull off.

00:24:45.116 --> 00:24:48.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so multilateralism
in theory is good,

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:51.656
in practice, you have to
always worry that that's,

00:24:51.656 --> 00:24:55.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's almost like a Chinese
communist decoy tactic

00:24:55.527 --> 00:24:57.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
where they're
intentionally encouraging

00:24:57.395 --> 00:24:58.763 align:start size:84% position:16%
us to be multilateral
because they know

00:24:58.763 --> 00:24:59.831
that will go super slow.

00:24:59.831 --> 00:25:01.399
- Right.

00:25:01.399 --> 00:25:02.667
Basically like having
the UN do anything-

00:25:02.667 --> 00:25:03.935
- Or the WTO, or all these,

00:25:03.935 --> 00:25:06.538
all these multinational
agencies that have been,

00:25:06.538 --> 00:25:08.173
you know, semi hijacked.

00:25:08.173 --> 00:25:09.441 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- So, so yeah.

00:25:09.441 --> 00:25:10.876
But I think, yeah,
I think there are,

00:25:11.743 --> 00:25:13.979
there are ways in which, yeah,

00:25:13.979 --> 00:25:18.283 align:start size:97% position:3%
one should start with
rethinking it on a US level

00:25:18.283 --> 00:25:22.120
and then it's definitely
something we need to bring

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:23.622
our allies into.

00:25:23.622 --> 00:25:26.925
- Do you think we have enough
sort of, not mental acumen,

00:25:26.925 --> 00:25:29.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
but do we have enough
like juice left in America

00:25:29.961 --> 00:25:31.796
to tackle things properly?

00:25:31.796 --> 00:25:34.399
I think that's what a lot of
people are feeling right now,

00:25:34.399 --> 00:25:36.701
that the incompetency
is so across the board,

00:25:36.701 --> 00:25:39.271
and Biden is so either
mentally compromised

00:25:39.271 --> 00:25:41.773
or has the wrong ideas, or
is staffed the wrong way,

00:25:41.773 --> 00:25:43.241
or whatever you wanna call that,

00:25:43.241 --> 00:25:45.677
that we just don't
have enough left

00:25:45.677 --> 00:25:48.313 align:start size:84% position:16%
to do the right
thing in the world.

00:25:48.313 --> 00:25:49.214
- You know, we.

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:53.685
- At least as it stands now.

00:25:53.685 --> 00:25:54.920
- There are always
worries that we have,

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:56.421
that we're exhausted.

00:25:56.421 --> 00:25:58.990
But I kind of wonder whether
this is just sort of the baby

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:02.360 align:start size:97% position:3%
boomer narrative where,
you know, the boomers were-

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:03.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I hope so.
- They were this very

00:26:03.862 --> 00:26:06.865
big generation, and then the
country was always defined

00:26:06.865 --> 00:26:09.034
by the age the boomers were.

00:26:09.034 --> 00:26:11.102
So the 1950s was this
innocent childhood time

00:26:11.102 --> 00:26:12.938
because the boomers
were 10 years old,

00:26:12.938 --> 00:26:17.108
and the late sixties was
this great youth movement

00:26:17.108 --> 00:26:19.411
because the boomers
were all in college,

00:26:19.411 --> 00:26:21.846
and the 1980s, the
boomers were yuppies,

00:26:21.846 --> 00:26:23.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
and now the boomers
are all retired

00:26:23.882 --> 00:26:26.117
and angry old people and then-

00:26:26.117 --> 00:26:27.586
- Or hanging on, or hanging on.

00:26:27.586 --> 00:26:29.020
- And then that somehow,

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:32.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
that somehow is the
template for the whole US.

00:26:32.457 --> 00:26:34.359
So I, you know, I think,

00:26:34.359 --> 00:26:35.794 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I think the
complicated answer is,

00:26:35.794 --> 00:26:37.629 align:start position:0%
there's some truth to it because
the boomers have dominated

00:26:37.629 --> 00:26:40.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
our society and
they're sort of in a,

00:26:40.899 --> 00:26:43.168 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a strange place right
now as a generation.

00:26:44.336 --> 00:26:46.304 align:start size:81% position:19%
But they're not
the whole society.

00:26:46.304 --> 00:26:47.472
We're not all boomers.

00:26:47.472 --> 00:26:49.441 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you think that's
a little bit of,

00:26:49.441 --> 00:26:51.543
because people are living longer

00:26:51.543 --> 00:26:53.812
and medicine has been good and
technology has enabled people

00:26:53.812 --> 00:26:56.114
to be sort of functional longer,

00:26:56.114 --> 00:26:59.284 align:start size:97% position:3%
that now we're ruled by
octogenarians who, you know,

00:26:59.284 --> 00:27:00.719
basically should, you know,

00:27:00.719 --> 00:27:02.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you see Nancy Pelosi
up there, it's like,

00:27:02.921 --> 00:27:03.855
go with your grandchildren,

00:27:03.855 --> 00:27:05.323
go play with your grandchildren.

00:27:05.323 --> 00:27:06.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
You don't have to be out there
still, or Biden, you know,

00:27:06.858 --> 00:27:09.628
it's like they can't
let go because science

00:27:09.628 --> 00:27:11.763
in some ways has
kept them going.

00:27:11.763 --> 00:27:13.198
- It hasn't changed it,

00:27:13.198 --> 00:27:14.733
it's been frustratingly slow.

00:27:14.733 --> 00:27:17.969
I mean, we've had some, some
extension of life expectancy

00:27:17.969 --> 00:27:20.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
actually reversed the
last few years with COVID

00:27:20.905 --> 00:27:22.474 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the opioid
epidemic, et cetera.

00:27:22.474 --> 00:27:24.609
But, but no, I think the main,

00:27:24.609 --> 00:27:27.245
the main dynamic
was you haven't,

00:27:27.245 --> 00:27:30.081
you never had a generation
like the boomers, you know.

00:27:30.081 --> 00:27:31.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm, you know, I'm Gen X.
- Yeah.

00:27:31.816 --> 00:27:33.485
- You know, there
are millennials

00:27:33.485 --> 00:27:35.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
and there's some generational
sensibility you can tell,

00:27:35.654 --> 00:27:38.289 align:start size:97% position:3%
or silent generation, there's
some generational story

00:27:38.289 --> 00:27:39.991
you can tell around other
people, but the, you know,

00:27:39.991 --> 00:27:43.261
the generation with a really
strong identity is the boomers.

00:27:43.261 --> 00:27:44.996 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think it's, there
were just so many of them.

00:27:44.996 --> 00:27:47.132
It was like in 1946,

00:27:47.132 --> 00:27:49.567 align:start size:97% position:3%
there were 20% more kids
born than 1945 or something.

00:27:49.567 --> 00:27:51.770
It was like a step function up,

00:27:51.770 --> 00:27:54.172
and then you get the
birth control pill
in the early sixties

00:27:54.172 --> 00:27:56.007
and you have fewer babies.

00:27:56.007 --> 00:27:58.376
And so it was just, it
was just a lot of people.

00:27:58.376 --> 00:27:59.878
- Where does that put us,

00:27:59.878 --> 00:28:02.514
the Gen Xers that seemingly
should be doing the thing

00:28:02.514 --> 00:28:05.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
right now and I suppose
in some cases we are, but,

00:28:05.717 --> 00:28:08.053
but really are the missing
generation in an odd way.

00:28:08.053 --> 00:28:10.555
We focus on boomers
and then, you know,

00:28:10.555 --> 00:28:12.057 align:start size:84% position:16%
millennials or Zoomers
or whatever it is.

00:28:12.057 --> 00:28:14.159
Like we've sort of missed
the people that are between

00:28:14.159 --> 00:28:16.594 align:start size:84% position:16%
say 40 and, you
know, late fifties.

00:28:16.594 --> 00:28:18.363 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, I've all these resentful
Gen X things I can say.

00:28:18.363 --> 00:28:20.231
But no, I think there's,

00:28:20.231 --> 00:28:22.133
there's probably some narrative

00:28:22.133 --> 00:28:27.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
where it's a smaller group
and so there's a risk

00:28:28.273 --> 00:28:29.641 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you end up being
sort of left out.

00:28:29.641 --> 00:28:30.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I think
there's some things

00:28:30.575 --> 00:28:31.643
where we did perfectly fine.

00:28:31.643 --> 00:28:33.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- I mean, we had our,

00:28:33.278 --> 00:28:35.647 align:start size:97% position:3%
we had our share of, you
know, Olympic gold medalists

00:28:35.647 --> 00:28:36.915 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you get those
at a certain age.

00:28:36.915 --> 00:28:39.517
And we were at the right
age at a certain point.

00:28:39.517 --> 00:28:41.453
You know, it's,

00:28:41.453 --> 00:28:45.423
we had 28 years of
boomer presidents,

00:28:45.423 --> 00:28:46.691
and I sometimes wonder whether

00:28:46.691 --> 00:28:48.059
we're ever gonna have
a Gen X president,

00:28:48.059 --> 00:28:49.394
which is just, it's not enough.

00:28:49.394 --> 00:28:51.596 align:start size:88% position:13%
And maybe you just skip
to the millennials.

00:28:51.596 --> 00:28:55.400
So, you know, the Silicon
Valley story in the 1990s

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:58.203
was the internet companies
were started by Gen X people

00:28:58.203 --> 00:29:01.539 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then somehow bought
out, taken over by boomers.

00:29:01.539 --> 00:29:03.208
And that's sort of what happened

00:29:03.208 --> 00:29:05.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
to almost all the
companies in the nineties.

00:29:05.076 --> 00:29:07.278
And then the boomers probably
had a healthier relationship

00:29:07.278 --> 00:29:08.780 align:start size:84% position:16%
with the millennials
where it was,

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:10.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
those were, the millennials
were their kids.

00:29:10.448 --> 00:29:12.584
And so they were, they
were a little bit nicer

00:29:12.584 --> 00:29:13.818
to the millennials
than they were to us.

00:29:13.818 --> 00:29:14.686
We were sort of more
their competitors.

00:29:14.686 --> 00:29:15.620
- Wow.
- So.

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:17.021
- We were their replacements.

00:29:17.021 --> 00:29:19.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
- When PayPal got
acquired by eBay in 2002,

00:29:21.126 --> 00:29:22.193
and it was sort of
this boomer company,

00:29:22.193 --> 00:29:24.829
and we were this Gen X company,

00:29:24.829 --> 00:29:28.066 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of my friends, David
Sack said, you know,

00:29:28.066 --> 00:29:30.301 align:start size:84% position:16%
if it would be a movie
it would be called,

00:29:30.301 --> 00:29:31.569
"Meet the Parents,"

00:29:31.569 --> 00:29:32.570
sort of this stodgy
older people company

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:34.139
was gonna clearly not be fun.

00:29:34.139 --> 00:29:35.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- When they took over.

00:29:35.607 --> 00:29:39.410 align:start size:97% position:3%
But actually you need a,
you need sort of a word for,

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:41.646 align:start size:88% position:13%
for people who are half
a generation older,

00:29:41.646 --> 00:29:44.783 align:start position:0%
not related to you and are
gonna be a lot less nice to you

00:29:44.783 --> 00:29:46.618
than your parents, and so.

00:29:46.618 --> 00:29:47.886
- Right, we need, we do
need a word for that.

00:29:47.886 --> 00:29:49.387
- So I think that it
would be more like,

00:29:49.387 --> 00:29:52.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
I dunno, Meg Whitman would
be like, more meet the,

00:29:54.292 --> 00:29:55.894
the evil young stepmother.

00:29:55.894 --> 00:29:57.562
- Right, right.

00:29:57.562 --> 00:29:59.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
So actually since
you mentioned Sacks,

00:29:59.097 --> 00:30:00.899
do you find it interesting if
you were to look back 20 years

00:30:00.899 --> 00:30:02.033
ago and boy, you know,

00:30:02.033 --> 00:30:03.868 align:start size:88% position:13%
Elon's doing everything
he's doing now.

00:30:03.868 --> 00:30:05.403
You've done incredible things.

00:30:05.403 --> 00:30:08.139
You know, Sacks is becoming
an outspoken political voice.

00:30:08.139 --> 00:30:09.374
You know, really anti-war.

00:30:09.374 --> 00:30:10.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
He's one of the people
leading that thing,

00:30:10.875 --> 00:30:13.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
that this crew of you know,
the PayPal mafia, so to speak.

00:30:13.678 --> 00:30:17.949
You guys are all still
in the mix in a odd way.

00:30:17.949 --> 00:30:19.184
Is there something special

00:30:19.184 --> 00:30:20.952
about what was going
on there 20 years ago?

00:30:20.952 --> 00:30:21.886 align:start size:72% position:28%
- It was.
- Or it's more

00:30:21.886 --> 00:30:22.821
than 20 years at this point.

00:30:22.821 --> 00:30:25.290
- You know, it's always hard to,

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:26.257
it's hard to tell the story.

00:30:26.257 --> 00:30:27.292
It was, it was,

00:30:30.762 --> 00:30:32.630 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't think we really
appreciated it at the time,

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:34.599 align:start size:97% position:3%
but yeah, it was a
phenomenal group of people.

00:30:34.599 --> 00:30:37.001 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's always, there's
always a sense where PayPal

00:30:37.001 --> 00:30:40.638
didn't really succeed in
that big a way, you know,

00:30:40.638 --> 00:30:42.574 align:start size:84% position:16%
it was a successful
exit in 2002,

00:30:42.574 --> 00:30:44.576
it was you know, one and a half
billion acquisition by eBay.

00:30:44.576 --> 00:30:46.344
But it didn't, you know,

00:30:46.344 --> 00:30:49.914
we couldn't figure out how to
run the business on our own.

00:30:49.914 --> 00:30:51.449 align:start size:84% position:16%
It made sense to
combine it with eBay

00:30:51.449 --> 00:30:52.417
for all sorts of reasons.

00:30:52.417 --> 00:30:53.985
It was, you know, you know,

00:30:53.985 --> 00:30:56.321
in some ways a depressing,
but a very rational thing-

00:30:56.321 --> 00:30:57.956 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Hmm.
- To do, and then-

00:30:57.956 --> 00:30:59.457
- Did you feel that at the time?

00:30:59.457 --> 00:31:01.192
That maybe you wanted to
hold on a little bit longer

00:31:01.192 --> 00:31:02.427
or something like that?

00:31:02.427 --> 00:31:05.263 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It, we didn't, it
was hard to see a path

00:31:05.263 --> 00:31:06.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
to an independent
business where, you know,

00:31:06.731 --> 00:31:09.601 align:start size:97% position:3%
eBay had the store and we were
running the cash registers

00:31:09.601 --> 00:31:11.803
and the people running the
store were trying to figure

00:31:11.803 --> 00:31:13.504 align:start size:97% position:3%
out how to get their own cash
register machines to work

00:31:13.504 --> 00:31:15.340 align:start position:0%
and figured it out one time
we'd be sort of out of business

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:17.709 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then the ways you
could gradually diversify

00:31:17.709 --> 00:31:21.246 align:start position:0%
away from eBay and, but it
took like a decade in practice.

00:31:21.246 --> 00:31:24.115
So I think, you know,
I think the combination

00:31:24.115 --> 00:31:25.617
made a lot of sense,

00:31:25.617 --> 00:31:27.752
but then it somehow short
circuited the business,

00:31:27.752 --> 00:31:31.055
whereas, you know, so many
of the other tech companies

00:31:31.055 --> 00:31:33.358 align:start size:81% position:19%
just scaled and
scaled and scaled,

00:31:33.358 --> 00:31:35.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
which like the Google history
or something like that.

00:31:35.994 --> 00:31:38.296
And that would've
been, you know,

00:31:38.296 --> 00:31:40.365
a far more successful version,

00:31:40.365 --> 00:31:43.868
but probably would've
done less, you know,

00:31:43.868 --> 00:31:47.805
if you, if you were,
if you had, you know,

00:31:47.805 --> 00:31:50.008
if you'd got gotten on
board the Google rocket

00:31:50.008 --> 00:31:52.310
at the right time, you should
have just never gotten off.

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:53.678
- Right.

00:31:53.678 --> 00:31:54.812 align:start size:88% position:13%
When you see the
frustrations that people

00:31:54.812 --> 00:31:56.381
have with these things,

00:31:56.381 --> 00:31:57.782
you know, the sort of lack
of trust in these things,

00:31:57.782 --> 00:32:00.285 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, is the government
working to silence

00:32:00.285 --> 00:32:01.619
you on Twitter?

00:32:01.619 --> 00:32:03.321 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or how is Google manipulating
the search results

00:32:03.321 --> 00:32:04.589
or all of these things?

00:32:04.589 --> 00:32:07.191
Do you also see those
as inevitable problems

00:32:07.191 --> 00:32:09.027 align:start size:84% position:16%
that were gonna happen
with these things?

00:32:09.027 --> 00:32:12.030
The reason I ask is I heard
you give a talk at NatCon,

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:14.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
you gave the keynote
speech last year.

00:32:14.232 --> 00:32:16.234 align:start size:97% position:3%
And one of the things you said
was that nobody represents

00:32:16.234 --> 00:32:18.603 align:start size:84% position:16%
the individual at
these big conferences.

00:32:18.603 --> 00:32:20.405 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I sort of think
that's the same problem

00:32:20.405 --> 00:32:21.639
that we have with tech.

00:32:21.639 --> 00:32:23.308
Nobody represents the
individual anymore.

00:32:23.308 --> 00:32:25.910 align:start size:88% position:13%
We just have these
giant corporations that,

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:28.813 align:start size:97% position:3%
or these giant tech companies
that make decisions.

00:32:28.813 --> 00:32:30.615 align:start size:84% position:16%
You cannot get
somebody on the phone.

00:32:30.615 --> 00:32:34.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
You can't actually
communicate as yourself.

00:32:34.485 --> 00:32:36.487 align:start position:0%
You, there is, you know, there's
a business version of it,

00:32:36.487 --> 00:32:37.755
something like that.

00:32:37.755 --> 00:32:41.125
- Yeah, there probably
are all kinds of ways

00:32:41.125 --> 00:32:43.227
they have biases
in that direction.

00:32:43.227 --> 00:32:45.930
You know, there's
always Nom Chomsky,

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:47.732
the communist MIT professor.

00:32:47.732 --> 00:32:49.734
I always like to quote him
on this where he says that,

00:32:49.734 --> 00:32:52.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, the Republicans
are the parties,

00:32:52.437 --> 00:32:55.406
the party of business, but
the Democrats discriminate.

00:32:55.406 --> 00:32:57.075
The Democrats are the
party of big business.

00:32:57.075 --> 00:32:59.344 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- And there's sort

00:32:59.344 --> 00:33:00.611
of like a center left.

00:33:00.611 --> 00:33:02.480
- Look at you, quoting a
communist, there you go.

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:04.315 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, you know-
- Every now and again.

00:33:04.315 --> 00:33:06.150
- Entirely wrong about
things or, you know.

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:08.987
But, there's sort of a
central left sensibility

00:33:08.987 --> 00:33:13.992
where, you know, basically big
businesses can be regulated.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:17.028
They'll follow all the rules.

00:33:17.028 --> 00:33:19.364
Small businesses, you know,

00:33:19.364 --> 00:33:20.999 align:start size:84% position:16%
they often make a
little bit more money

00:33:20.999 --> 00:33:24.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
by being a gray area,
not following the
rules to the letter.

00:33:26.304 --> 00:33:29.407
And so there is probably just
this structural anti small

00:33:29.407 --> 00:33:33.277
business bias that's
you know, political,

00:33:33.277 --> 00:33:36.881 align:start size:97% position:3%
regulatory, cultural,
partisan, that's very deep.

00:33:36.881 --> 00:33:37.815
- Yeah, were you
shocked how obvious

00:33:37.815 --> 00:33:39.283
that became during COVID?

00:33:39.283 --> 00:33:42.020
I mean, where, you know,
Target could stay open for,

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:43.554
you know, the big box store,

00:33:43.554 --> 00:33:44.989
but the mom and pop that was
selling the exact same thing

00:33:44.989 --> 00:33:46.257
next door got closed.

00:33:46.257 --> 00:33:47.859
That shows the bias
right there, right?

00:33:47.859 --> 00:33:51.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
The system just
kind of eliminated a
certain set of people.

00:33:51.429 --> 00:33:54.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes, I think, I
mean, I think it was,

00:33:56.034 --> 00:33:58.403
yeah, it was a, I mean a
dramatic shift in terms

00:33:58.403 --> 00:34:01.072 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the power of big relative
to small businesses.

00:34:01.072 --> 00:34:03.608
And it probably, I dunno,

00:34:03.608 --> 00:34:06.411
I think in some ways COVID
surfaced all these realities

00:34:06.411 --> 00:34:08.846
that had been there
for a long time.

00:34:08.846 --> 00:34:10.381 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- And yeah, this was,

00:34:10.381 --> 00:34:12.583
this was the institutional
center left establishment

00:34:12.583 --> 00:34:13.885
in this country.

00:34:13.885 --> 00:34:15.887
You know, it's good
with big business,

00:34:15.887 --> 00:34:18.156
it's anti, it's very
anti small business.

00:34:18.156 --> 00:34:20.224
- How did you fight some of
that with your businesses

00:34:20.224 --> 00:34:21.726 align:start size:88% position:13%
during COVID and
figuring out, you know,

00:34:21.726 --> 00:34:24.562
were people gonna
work from home or,

00:34:24.562 --> 00:34:27.198
or just all of the nonsense
that everybody dealt with?

00:34:27.198 --> 00:34:29.400 align:start size:97% position:3%
Did you try to give as much
power to your employees

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:31.102
and say, do what
you gotta do or?

00:34:31.102 --> 00:34:33.571
- Well, you know, most of the-

00:34:33.571 --> 00:34:34.505
- 'Cause even now
a lot of the people

00:34:34.505 --> 00:34:35.940
still don't want to come back.

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:37.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's one of the problems
that Elon's having.

00:34:37.141 --> 00:34:41.512
- Most of the tech
companies were pretty

00:34:43.281 --> 00:34:45.550 align:start size:81% position:19%
well positioned
to adapt to COVID,

00:34:45.550 --> 00:34:47.685
where, you know, if you're,

00:34:47.685 --> 00:34:50.321
there were sort of ways you
could do the remote work.

00:34:52.423 --> 00:34:54.659 align:start size:88% position:13%
You could work remotely,
do things like that.

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:58.096 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it seemingly didn't
hurt the business too much.

00:34:58.096 --> 00:34:59.730
And then of course there was,

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:00.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
there was a way
where COVID shifted

00:35:00.832 --> 00:35:02.366
a lot to the internet.

00:35:02.366 --> 00:35:04.802 align:start size:97% position:3%
So a lot of the tech companies
in which I'm involved,

00:35:04.802 --> 00:35:08.039
you know, got a, got a big
temporary boost from COVID

00:35:08.039 --> 00:35:09.507
even though, you know,

00:35:09.507 --> 00:35:12.510
maybe they, maybe they actually
got, you know, more bloated,

00:35:12.510 --> 00:35:14.679
less well managed in the
last two, three years.

00:35:14.679 --> 00:35:15.813
And that's what I worry about.

00:35:15.813 --> 00:35:17.014 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- So it was,

00:35:17.014 --> 00:35:18.683
it was actually sort
of a windfall for them.

00:35:18.683 --> 00:35:20.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then the question
is just did they,

00:35:20.685 --> 00:35:22.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
did they really take
advantage of it,

00:35:22.753 --> 00:35:26.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
or did they get even more
dysfunctional in various ways?

00:35:26.257 --> 00:35:28.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Do you think more
people in the tech world,

00:35:28.593 --> 00:35:29.994
or maybe even in
the political world

00:35:29.994 --> 00:35:32.430
actually think like
you to some degree,

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:36.000
but because of the way
we, the hive mind is,

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:38.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
or the globalist movement
or whatever it is,

00:35:38.069 --> 00:35:39.604
they just sort of
always go to that.

00:35:39.604 --> 00:35:41.205
But I think, you know,

00:35:41.205 --> 00:35:42.673
if you privately sat
down with these people

00:35:42.673 --> 00:35:44.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
about what their real
beliefs in the individual

00:35:44.509 --> 00:35:47.044
are in capitalism
in these things.

00:35:47.044 --> 00:35:48.513
- Directionally yes.

00:35:48.513 --> 00:35:51.716
But I think it, I always
wonder if it actually works

00:35:51.716 --> 00:35:53.384
if you can't say it.

00:35:53.384 --> 00:35:58.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, so yes, surely it's almost
the definition of political

00:35:58.022 --> 00:36:00.825
correctness that
it distorts things.

00:36:00.825 --> 00:36:01.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- And that there are

00:36:01.959 --> 00:36:03.427
all sorts of people who are,

00:36:03.427 --> 00:36:05.730 align:start position:0%
people are less politically
correct than they appear to be,

00:36:05.730 --> 00:36:07.765 align:start size:97% position:3%
because political correctness
is about appearances.

00:36:07.765 --> 00:36:10.535 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then the reality's
always that people

00:36:10.535 --> 00:36:12.670
are gonna think it's
a little bit crazy.

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:14.405 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, there probably
are a lot of parents

00:36:14.405 --> 00:36:16.774 align:start size:84% position:16%
who think the schools
went very crazy.

00:36:16.774 --> 00:36:20.878 align:start size:88% position:13%
But, but then if you
feel like you can't talk

00:36:20.878 --> 00:36:22.980
about it or articulate it,

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:26.384
it's not going to be that
well formed a view at all.

00:36:26.384 --> 00:36:30.721
And so that's, and so I think
the political correctness is,

00:36:30.721 --> 00:36:32.957
is real to the extent
it just stops people

00:36:32.957 --> 00:36:34.659
from saying things.

00:36:34.659 --> 00:36:37.528
You don't actually get
to a very considered

00:36:37.528 --> 00:36:38.863
non-politically correct opinion.

00:36:38.863 --> 00:36:40.398
- Right, it's interesting,

00:36:40.398 --> 00:36:41.532
'cause that also then
gets to the stagnation

00:36:41.532 --> 00:36:43.034
part that you're talking about.

00:36:43.034 --> 00:36:44.635
If people can't talk about
what the actual issues are,

00:36:44.635 --> 00:36:46.170 align:start size:84% position:16%
then you really
don't have to wonder

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:49.540
why we're so stagnated and
why we got 140 characters

00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:51.042 align:start size:88% position:13%
instead of flying cars.
- Sure.

00:36:51.042 --> 00:36:52.710
There's probably some way
all these things, yeah,

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:54.612
all these things are linked.

00:36:54.612 --> 00:36:57.515
But, but yeah,

00:36:57.515 --> 00:37:01.686
I think if we live in a society

00:37:01.686 --> 00:37:03.854
where there are an
awful lot of topics

00:37:03.854 --> 00:37:06.857 align:start size:88% position:13%
that are somewhat off
limits, you know, where,

00:37:06.857 --> 00:37:08.826
you know, and if we
think about science,

00:37:08.826 --> 00:37:12.863
let's think about sort of
freedom of speech or debate

00:37:12.863 --> 00:37:14.465
in the area of science.

00:37:14.465 --> 00:37:17.101
And I always think you
can describe science

00:37:17.101 --> 00:37:20.371
as involving a two
front war in theory.

00:37:20.371 --> 00:37:22.740
Should be a two
front war against

00:37:22.740 --> 00:37:25.476
excessive dogmatism and
excessive skepticism.

00:37:25.476 --> 00:37:28.479
So excessive dogmatism in the
17th, 18th century context,

00:37:28.479 --> 00:37:31.349
it's like the Catholic church
or it's this sort of decayed

00:37:31.349 --> 00:37:35.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
Aristotelianism and you
know, a scientist, you know,

00:37:35.386 --> 00:37:38.389 align:start size:88% position:13%
needs to think for
themselves and challenge

00:37:38.389 --> 00:37:42.893
the sort of ossified dogmas
or ossified metaphysics.

00:37:42.893 --> 00:37:45.997 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you just do an experiment,
you think for yourself.

00:37:45.997 --> 00:37:48.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
But then you also
can't be a scientist

00:37:48.232 --> 00:37:50.101
if you're too skeptical.

00:37:50.101 --> 00:37:51.969
So if I don't think you exist,

00:37:51.969 --> 00:37:54.171
I think you're just
a simulation or-

00:37:54.171 --> 00:37:55.740 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- Everything's fake.

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.074
Nothing's real.

00:37:57.074 --> 00:37:59.343
I'm just in a brain being,

00:37:59.343 --> 00:38:01.345
I'm just a brain in a
vat being manipulated

00:38:01.345 --> 00:38:03.114 align:start size:88% position:13%
by mad scientists.
- That's why I got bored

00:38:03.114 --> 00:38:04.548
of interviewing atheists.

00:38:04.548 --> 00:38:05.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
- That's not a good
world for science either.

00:38:05.883 --> 00:38:07.084 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- So you can't

00:38:07.084 --> 00:38:08.853
be too dogmatic, you
can't be too skeptical.

00:38:08.853 --> 00:38:11.155 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- And sort of probably

00:38:11.155 --> 00:38:13.157
healthy version of
science cuts against

00:38:13.157 --> 00:38:16.727
both excess dogmatism
and excess skepticism.

00:38:16.727 --> 00:38:21.632 align:start size:97% position:3%
But my scoring is, it's all
anti skepticism at this point.

00:38:21.632 --> 00:38:24.669
The scientific establishment,
it's all circling the wagons.

00:38:24.669 --> 00:38:26.103 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- And we have

00:38:26.103 --> 00:38:27.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
a climate change skeptic,
we have a, you know,

00:38:27.638 --> 00:38:29.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
you can't be skeptic
of, you know,

00:38:29.707 --> 00:38:31.075
you can't be a vaccine skeptic,

00:38:31.075 --> 00:38:33.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
you can't be a
skeptic of anything.

00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:35.246 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so it's all
against skepticism,

00:38:35.246 --> 00:38:37.515 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is of course the
exact opposite of, you know,

00:38:37.515 --> 00:38:39.517 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's say a children's
science book

00:38:39.517 --> 00:38:41.986 align:start size:94% position:6%
would be that a scientist
thinks for themselves

00:38:41.986 --> 00:38:45.122
and is against dogmatism,
not against skepticism.

00:38:45.122 --> 00:38:46.557
- So what do we do?

00:38:46.557 --> 00:38:47.992
What do we do to
break outta that?

00:38:47.992 --> 00:38:48.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's 80% anti dogmatism,
20% anti skepticism.

00:38:48.793 --> 00:38:50.061
That's healthy science.

00:38:50.061 --> 00:38:51.595
We're in a world where
it's a hundred percent

00:38:51.595 --> 00:38:52.830
anti skepticism.

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:55.499 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's a tell that
it's hyper dogmatic

00:38:55.499 --> 00:38:57.301
and that the scientists, no,

00:38:57.301 --> 00:39:00.671
the scientists can't talk
freely about the science.

00:39:00.671 --> 00:39:02.039
And if you have, you
know, if you have,

00:39:02.039 --> 00:39:03.140
if you have dissenting views,

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:04.308
you better keep them to yourself

00:39:04.308 --> 00:39:05.910 align:start size:88% position:13%
or your government
funding will get cut off

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:08.145
and they're, you know, they're
all in the sort of government

00:39:08.145 --> 00:39:09.780
welfare or something like that.

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:11.248 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I mean, look at the
last three years of COVID

00:39:11.248 --> 00:39:12.850 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think you pretty much
get your answer, right?

00:39:12.850 --> 00:39:17.855
- A shockingly narrow range of
discourse allowed in science.

00:39:18.956 --> 00:39:20.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Was there a
moment during COVID-

00:39:20.257 --> 00:39:21.092
- Science in scare quotes.

00:39:21.092 --> 00:39:22.259
- Yeah, right.

00:39:22.259 --> 00:39:23.661 align:start size:84% position:16%
So was there a
moment during COVID

00:39:23.661 --> 00:39:26.197 align:start size:97% position:3%
where you realized how
dysregulating that effect was?

00:39:26.197 --> 00:39:28.899
That you couldn't get a counter?

00:39:28.899 --> 00:39:30.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I saw you a
couple times during COVID

00:39:30.334 --> 00:39:31.535
for dinners and things,

00:39:31.535 --> 00:39:33.137
and it was like we
weren't wearing masks

00:39:33.137 --> 00:39:34.372
and we were sitting there,

00:39:34.372 --> 00:39:35.606
I don't even know
if you were allowed

00:39:35.606 --> 00:39:37.041
to have people at your house.

00:39:37.041 --> 00:39:39.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, but humans continued
and yet the machine

00:39:39.310 --> 00:39:40.811
just kept telling you, no,

00:39:40.811 --> 00:39:43.247
stay in your house and wear
the masks and get the vax,

00:39:43.247 --> 00:39:44.281
and do-do-do.

00:39:45.349 --> 00:39:47.351
- I had a lot of skepticism

00:39:47.351 --> 00:39:49.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
about all these things
before I would say.

00:39:49.687 --> 00:39:50.855 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- Or the skepticism

00:39:50.855 --> 00:39:52.857 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the excessive
dogmatism of science.

00:39:52.857 --> 00:39:56.160 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- And I think,

00:39:57.294 --> 00:40:01.766
I think that had, but
yes, it was still,

00:40:01.766 --> 00:40:04.602
it was still strike, it was
like you had these, I dunno,

00:40:04.602 --> 00:40:05.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
you had these public
health officials,

00:40:05.903 --> 00:40:07.438 align:start size:84% position:16%
all these people were,
it was just, again,

00:40:07.438 --> 00:40:11.108
the opportunity to really
to push it in a conformist

00:40:11.108 --> 00:40:12.243
standardized way.

00:40:12.243 --> 00:40:14.311 align:start size:88% position:13%
It was, what was
extraordinary about it,

00:40:14.311 --> 00:40:17.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
wasn't just the dogmatism
and the uniformity,

00:40:17.882 --> 00:40:20.584 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it was, it was the
Orwellian character

00:40:20.584 --> 00:40:24.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
where we pivoted radically
from black to white,

00:40:24.922 --> 00:40:27.525
A to not A and so,
you know, it was,

00:40:27.525 --> 00:40:30.494 align:start size:88% position:13%
it was, I'm not gonna
get, the history was so,

00:40:30.494 --> 00:40:32.096
it was so many twists and turns,

00:40:32.096 --> 00:40:33.164
it's hard to even
keep it straight.

00:40:33.164 --> 00:40:35.065
But I believe October, 2020,

00:40:35.065 --> 00:40:36.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
it was still Kamala
Harris saying

00:40:36.801 --> 00:40:38.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
that she would never
take a vaccine.

00:40:38.035 --> 00:40:40.004 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- A Trump vaccine.

00:40:40.004 --> 00:40:41.205
- A Trump vaccine, yeah.

00:40:41.205 --> 00:40:43.207 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And then, you know,
and then when I-

00:40:43.207 --> 00:40:45.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That you literally
shouldn't trust the agencies

00:40:45.576 --> 00:40:46.944 align:start size:88% position:13%
because he has
something to do with it.

00:40:46.944 --> 00:40:48.479
That's what she said.

00:40:48.479 --> 00:40:51.182
- And then, you know,
and then a year later

00:40:51.182 --> 00:40:54.151
it's like you're a really crazy
person if you don't get one.

00:40:54.151 --> 00:40:58.022 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we had these sort
of Orwellian twists
in the narrative.

00:40:58.022 --> 00:40:59.723 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- You know, there was,

00:40:59.723 --> 00:41:01.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
there was the thing where
originally the masks

00:41:01.225 --> 00:41:04.728
didn't work because they
were trying to lie to save

00:41:04.728 --> 00:41:06.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
the mask for the hospital
workers or something.

00:41:06.597 --> 00:41:08.098 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Right.
- And then you

00:41:08.098 --> 00:41:09.066
pivoted on that.

00:41:09.066 --> 00:41:10.568
There was, you know, anyway,

00:41:10.568 --> 00:41:12.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
there was all these, these
crazy twists and turns.

00:41:12.470 --> 00:41:14.705
There was, you know,
there was the initial,

00:41:14.705 --> 00:41:17.741 align:start size:97% position:3%
the very initial one where,
you know, it was just,

00:41:17.741 --> 00:41:20.978
we shouldn't shut the border
because that's anti-globalist.

00:41:20.978 --> 00:41:24.515
And then when Trump, you know,

00:41:25.549 --> 00:41:28.519 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then when
President Trump didn't,

00:41:28.519 --> 00:41:29.487
wasn't restrictive enough,

00:41:29.487 --> 00:41:30.788
then somehow it all shifted

00:41:30.788 --> 00:41:33.224
into the sort of
pure nanny state.

00:41:33.224 --> 00:41:36.293
- Yeah, so it seems like
it all flipped almost,

00:41:36.293 --> 00:41:37.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think this is
what you're saying,

00:41:37.795 --> 00:41:39.330
it happened so quickly that we
almost couldn't react, like,

00:41:39.330 --> 00:41:42.132
so the same people who were
saying, my body, my choice,

00:41:42.132 --> 00:41:44.969
were the same ones yelling at
you that you must be injected

00:41:44.969 --> 00:41:46.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
with the thing I
wanna inject you with.

00:41:46.303 --> 00:41:48.539 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think that was
so dysregulating.

00:41:48.539 --> 00:41:50.574
- Sort of like, it was like,
you know, the sky is blue,

00:41:50.574 --> 00:41:52.209 align:start size:88% position:13%
but no, they were
saying the sky is green

00:41:52.209 --> 00:41:55.546
and then the sky is orange
and then the sky is yellow.

00:41:55.546 --> 00:42:00.284 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it was just this dizzying
shift in the dogmas.

00:42:00.284 --> 00:42:01.819
It wasn't like the Catholic
church in the Middle Ages

00:42:01.819 --> 00:42:03.454 align:start size:84% position:16%
where at least the
dogmas stayed the same

00:42:03.454 --> 00:42:04.488
for a few hundred years.

00:42:04.488 --> 00:42:06.023 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- They don't,

00:42:06.023 --> 00:42:07.124
they didn't change them
like every six months.

00:42:07.124 --> 00:42:08.158
- So what does that tell you,

00:42:08.158 --> 00:42:09.727
I guess this is a little bit

00:42:09.727 --> 00:42:11.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
of what you're doing
at Stanford now.

00:42:11.629 --> 00:42:14.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
What does that tell you
about people's belief systems

00:42:14.331 --> 00:42:15.733
and how they operate?

00:42:17.034 --> 00:42:18.335
- Well, it's, it is-

00:42:19.737 --> 00:42:21.639
- 'Cause they seem to believe
anything on any given day.

00:42:21.639 --> 00:42:23.507 align:start size:97% position:3%
You could almost, depending
on who was president

00:42:23.507 --> 00:42:26.043
and what their, you
know, party was,

00:42:26.043 --> 00:42:28.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
you could get virtually
anyone to say almost anything.

00:42:30.114 --> 00:42:32.550
- Yeah, I would say, I would,

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:34.919
I mean it's hard to do
sweeping generalizations

00:42:34.919 --> 00:42:38.289
about our society, but it's
striking how many things

00:42:38.289 --> 00:42:40.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
are not very well
thought through at all.

00:42:40.824 --> 00:42:42.660
And there are, yeah,
I think there are,

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.963
there's some set of things
where things are doctrinaire

00:42:45.963 --> 00:42:49.567 align:start size:97% position:3%
and dogmatic and then
they're all kinds of issues

00:42:49.567 --> 00:42:53.137 align:start size:84% position:16%
that barely even
register as problems

00:42:53.137 --> 00:42:54.438
and we don't even talk about.

00:42:54.438 --> 00:42:56.774
So I, yeah, I think
there's this, yeah,

00:42:56.774 --> 00:42:58.275
this official ideology,

00:42:58.275 --> 00:43:01.845
but it's almost like a
magic show hypnotic trick

00:43:01.845 --> 00:43:03.647 align:start size:88% position:13%
where you know, it
redirects our attention

00:43:03.647 --> 00:43:04.882
from other things.

00:43:04.882 --> 00:43:06.350
So people have, yeah,

00:43:06.350 --> 00:43:09.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
they have very well defined
opinions on the vaccine

00:43:09.787 --> 00:43:11.655
and those are sort
of officially set.

00:43:11.655 --> 00:43:14.825
But then if we talk about a
topic like tech stagnation

00:43:14.825 --> 00:43:17.494 align:start size:97% position:3%
or how fast are we developing
vaccines generally,

00:43:17.494 --> 00:43:20.764 align:start size:97% position:3%
how fast are we curing other
diseases besides COVID?

00:43:20.764 --> 00:43:22.433
That's something people
don't even think about.

00:43:22.433 --> 00:43:23.934
- Right.

00:43:23.934 --> 00:43:26.604
What are you thinking about
that maybe the average person

00:43:26.604 --> 00:43:28.138
isn't thinking about?

00:43:28.138 --> 00:43:29.907
Like if we're to get to the
other side of the stagnation

00:43:29.907 --> 00:43:31.775 align:start position:0%
and let's say we start breaking
through some of this stuff,

00:43:31.775 --> 00:43:34.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
which we will eventually,
some society has to,

00:43:34.211 --> 00:43:38.649
I think to some degree, what
should we be thinking about?

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:43.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, there are, I mean,
there are, there are a lot of,

00:43:43.487 --> 00:43:47.224 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are a lot of
different topics one could,

00:43:47.224 --> 00:43:50.461
I mean, I dunno, there's
some that I've, you know,

00:43:50.461 --> 00:43:52.663
I feel I've thought about,

00:43:54.064 --> 00:43:55.299
there's probably a small number

00:43:55.299 --> 00:43:57.901
that I always keep
coming back to, that.

00:43:57.901 --> 00:43:59.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
But-
- I got one I want you

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:00.738
to come back to, I'll
tell you in a second,

00:44:00.738 --> 00:44:02.239
but I wanna hear what you-

00:44:02.239 --> 00:44:06.477
- Probably the, you know,
the big one is always just,

00:44:07.911 --> 00:44:10.180
you know, I don't think
our society's progressing

00:44:10.180 --> 00:44:12.449 align:start size:84% position:16%
that quickly in that
many dimensions.

00:44:12.449 --> 00:44:14.251
Why has it slowed?

00:44:14.251 --> 00:44:15.352
What's gone wrong?

00:44:15.352 --> 00:44:16.787
Why has that happened?

00:44:16.787 --> 00:44:20.724 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's probably, that's
probably the big topic

00:44:21.925 --> 00:44:24.294 align:start size:84% position:16%
question theme that
I've come back to over

00:44:24.294 --> 00:44:26.830
the last two decades,
over and over again.

00:44:26.830 --> 00:44:29.400
And then there are, yeah,

00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:32.403 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's all sorts of different
answers one can get to.

00:44:33.771 --> 00:44:36.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
There probably are good
reasons for us to be so slow,

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:40.811
but I think it pushes you to
ask a lot of deep questions

00:44:40.811 --> 00:44:42.179
about our society,
that it would be good

00:44:42.179 --> 00:44:43.681
for us to think about more.

00:44:43.681 --> 00:44:45.616
- So the one that I don't
know if you're thinking

00:44:45.616 --> 00:44:46.884
about at all anymore,

00:44:46.884 --> 00:44:48.285
I suspect you are at
least at some level,

00:44:48.285 --> 00:44:50.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
is you were really
interested in seasteading

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:52.723 align:start size:88% position:13%
a couple probably what,
15 or so years ago,

00:44:52.723 --> 00:44:54.324
this idea that there could sort

00:44:54.324 --> 00:44:56.560
of be these libertarian utopias-

00:44:56.560 --> 00:44:58.095 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yes.
- Sort of international

00:44:58.095 --> 00:45:00.698
waters where people could
do experimental medication

00:45:00.698 --> 00:45:02.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
and operations and
things of this nature.

00:45:02.666 --> 00:45:05.369 align:start size:97% position:3%
To me, it feels like so
many people have such a lack

00:45:05.369 --> 00:45:06.970
of faith in the system,

00:45:06.970 --> 00:45:08.739 align:start size:88% position:13%
that there's an
opportunity there again.

00:45:08.739 --> 00:45:10.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
Does, is that registering
with you at all?

00:45:10.841 --> 00:45:12.576
Is it, do you feel like
the operation is gone?

00:45:12.576 --> 00:45:15.012
Has someone else picked up
maybe where you left off?

00:45:15.012 --> 00:45:16.947
- They are still trying to,

00:45:16.947 --> 00:45:19.083
trying to do it in various ways.

00:45:19.083 --> 00:45:22.119
